# project

- label: project is implemented in TypeScript and executes via the Bun runtime (oven/bun Docker base, bun.lock present)
  command: test -f bun.lock && grep -q "FROM oven/bun" Dockerfile

- label: Docker image builds successfully from Dockerfile (Bun-based)
  command: docker build --check -t facts-check . >/dev/null 2>&1

# domain
- an Adapter is a pluggable module exporting name and fetch(AdapterConfig): Promise<ContentItem[]>
- a ContentItem is the normalized content {id, title, url, source, timestamp: Date, body?}
- an AppConfig is the parsed YAML declaring adapters, pipelines, layout, and optional llm
- a Panel is a named dashboard section sourcing items from an adapter, pipeline or the 'all' sentinel via layout
- a Layout is a recursive tree of Panels and flex containers defining the server-rendered dashboard structure
- a Pipeline is a named processing step that reads prior sources, applies ordered transforms, and feeds panels
- a Transform is an ordered ingest-time operation such as filter, dedupe, keyword-score, llm-summarize or llm-merge
- a Scheduler manages periodic and manual refreshes of adapters and pipelines, persisting results to DB
- a ContentItemRow is a persisted ContentItem with panel_id, fetched_at, and optional LLM summary
- an LLMConfig is the optional block enabling pi-ai providers for summaries, filtering, ranking and merging
- an AppConfig declares Adapters, Pipelines and a Layout which together drive the Scheduler
- a Layout references Adapters or Pipelines (or 'all') as Panel sources; 'all' bypasses normal mapping for global recent view
- the Scheduler invokes Adapter fetch or Pipeline processing then applies ordered Transforms before persisting as ContentItemRows
- LLM-powered Transforms and summaries require a valid LLMConfig and model; otherwise they silently degrade to no-op
- ContentItemRows for a Panel are queried deduplicated (latest timestamp per url-norm or id) to render the Dashboard

# cli
- label: the CLI supports --config, --port, --preset/-P, --list-presets, --help, --version; unknown commands or options cause immediate exit with usage
  command: bun run src/cli.ts --help 2>&1 | grep -q "Usage:" && bun run src/cli.ts badcmd 2>&1 | grep -q "Unknown command"
  tags: [implemented]
- label: the CLI always chdirs to its own project root (package.json dir) before starting to ensure tsconfig, defaults and data/ are found regardless of invocation cwd
  command: grep -q "process.chdir(projectRoot)" src/cli.ts && grep -q "projectRoot = join(import.meta.dir, \"..\")" src/cli.ts
- label: CLI supports --preset <name> / -P <name> and --list-presets for bundled presets
  command: bun run src/cli.ts --list-presets 2>&1 | grep -q tech-news && bun run src/cli.ts --help 2>&1 | grep -q 'preset <name>'
  tags: [spec, implemented]

# config
- when no config file is present, loadConfig falls back to config.example.yaml then a default layout with a single 'all' panel
- config errors are always prefixed with 'config: ' and cause the process to exit 1 before the server starts
- AppConfig validation is exhaustive and path-specific: unknown top-level keys, missing required fields, invalid numbers/enums, duplicate names/IDs, bad pipeline source refs, and partial LLM creds all produce precise 'config: ...' errors at load time
- the special source value 'all' (and panels using it) bypasses adapter/pipeline name validation and sourceToPanels mapping; it queries global recent items instead of per-panel
- Panel IDs are stable 8-character hex (first of sha256 of JSON.stringify({panel, source, limit})) or explicit id from config; used for DB keys, refresh URLs, and sourceToPanels mapping
- environment variables in the form ${VAR} are recursively substituted in all string values of the YAML (including in llm.api_key, adapter params, etc.) before schema validation
- label: loadConfig resolves bare short preset names (tech-news, ml-ai, etc) to bundled /app/presets/config.*.yaml
  command: bun run src/cli.ts --preset tech-news --help 2>&1 | grep -q 'pace v'
  tags: [spec, implemented]

# scheduler
- scheduler errors for missing adapter types are prefixed 'scheduler: ' and cause startup to fail with exit 1
- the Scheduler starts an immediate fetch for each configured adapter on startup; pipelines are scheduled with a 5s initial delay; default refresh_interval is 15 minutes (minimum 1)

# server
- label: the server listens on port 7453 by default (or PORT env / --port); supports graceful shutdown on SIGTERM/SIGINT (stops scheduler, closes DB)
  command: grep -q "parsePort(process.env.PORT, 7453)" src/index.ts
- manual refresh via POST /refresh/:panel returns 502 with details if any source in the batch fails; success redirects to / with 303
- every response includes security headers: X-Content-Type-Options nosniff, X-Frame-Options DENY, Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin, CSP default-src self with unsafe-inline for styles
- GET /health returns JSON {status: "ok"}; GET /styles.css serves the static CSS with 1h cache

# adapters
- every Adapter must export a default object with .name (string) and .fetch(config: AdapterConfig): Promise<ContentItem[]>; discovery scans src/adapters/*.ts at runtime (excludes tests/types/index)
- Adapter fetch implementations that encounter network/HTTP/parse errors either throw (with `${name}: ` prefix + errorMessage) or console.warn and return []; the contract in types.ts documents the expectation for callers
- ContentItem ids are prefixed by adapter (e.g. 'hn:', 'rss:', 'github:') or 'pipeline:${name}:' to avoid collisions across sources; used as DB PK for upsert
- every ContentItem.timestamp is a valid Date (parse failures or missing dates fall back to new Date())

# db
- DB persistence uses SQLite with WAL mode and 5s busy_timeout; items are upserted by id (ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE); reads for UI and transforms always deduplicate keeping MAX(timestamp) per url-normalized key (or id if no url)
- old ContentItemRows are pruned daily (and on startup) if their fetched_at is older than 30 days; prune is independent of item timestamp

# transforms
- Transforms declared in an adapter or pipeline config are applied in exact declaration order during refresh via runPipeline; unknown transform types warn and are skipped (passthrough)
- dedupe transform supports strategies 'url' (exact), 'domain-normalized' (group by normalized host+path), 'title-similarity' (greedy Levenshtein); 'keep' (highest-score, earliest, latest) decides winner per group; logs removals

# llm
- llm-summarize, llm-filter, llm-rank, llm-merge transforms require LLMConfig + model in context; withLlmModel guard causes silent passthrough (no change to items or summaries) if no model configured
- LLM summaries (from llm-summarize or llm-merge) are persisted in ContentItemRow.summary and rendered in dashboard; replacePanelItems preserves prior summaries across transform runs

# layout
- the Dashboard is entirely server-rendered (Hono JSX to full HTML document with inline styles link); no client-side JavaScript is served or required
- layout containers switch to vertical stacking on screens <=768px via CSS media query regardless of YAML direction; panels use flex + scroll for content
- the dashboard no longer renders a top-level .header div containing h1 'pace' and a render-timestamp span @spec @implemented
- the rendered dashboard includes a footer element at the end of the body containing tiny caption text in the format 'Pace / <render datetime> UTC' @spec @implemented
- the 'Pace' word in the dashboard footer caption is a hyperlink (anchor element) pointing to https://github.com/av/pace (opens in new tab with appropriate rel) @spec @implemented
- the footer uses compact tiny styling (font-size ~0.65rem, muted text color, minimal vertical margins/padding) so it adds negligible height and makes the overall dashboard layout more compact than the previous header @spec @implemented
- the HTML document title remains 'pace'; only the visible header branding moves to the compact footer @spec @implemented
- dashboard body uses 1rem padding (tighter frame around panels) for more compact overall dashboard presentation @spec @implemented
- panel headers use 0.5rem 0.75rem padding (reduced from 0.75rem 1rem) for compact panel chrome @spec @implemented
- content items in panels use 0.5rem 0.75rem padding, 0.35rem meta gaps, 0.15rem title margin-bottom and 0.2rem summary margin-top for tighter vertical spacing @spec @implemented
- item titles in dashboard panels use 0.85rem font-size; summaries use 0.75rem font-size for higher information density in compact panels @spec @implemented
- panel action gaps reduced to 0.35rem, refresh button padding to 0.1rem 0.3rem, and empty-state padding to 1.5rem 1rem for consistent compact panel styling @spec @implemented
- dashboard panels use more compact styling overall (reduced paddings, margins, gaps and fonts inside panels and items) resulting in higher content density and less wasted vertical space compared to prior styling @spec @implemented
- when dashboard panels contain more items than fit their space, they display an internal scrollbar rather than expanding the dashboard page or causing the viewport to scroll @spec @implemented
- the dashboard root (html, body, .flex-root and top-level layout containers) is strictly constrained to the full viewport height using min-height:100vh + height:100% propagation and overflow:hidden on the document body; this gives .panel-body a definite height so it renders an internal vertical scrollbar for excess items instead of expanding the page @spec @implemented
- the .panel element (direct parent of .panel-body) receives explicit height:100% (in addition to flex:1) so the flex column inside it constrains .panel-body to a definite height smaller than its content; overflow-y:auto then produces a visible internal scrollbar for excess items @spec @implemented

# readme

## quick-start
- label: README Quick Start includes a docker run example that mounts a pre-built preset config
  command: grep -q "Quick start" README.md && grep -A 30 "## Quick start" README.md | grep -q "preset" && grep -A 30 "## Quick start" README.md | grep -q "config\..*\.yaml"
  tags: [spec, implemented]
- README Quick Start From source section states that Bun v1.3+ is required before the clone/install commands @spec @implemented
- README Quick Start instructs users that content populates after the initial adapter fetches once the server is listening @spec @implemented

## presets
- label: README contains a table of presets documenting all pre-built config files and their focus areas
  command: grep -q "^## Presets" README.md && grep -A 15 "^## Presets" README.md | grep -q "config\..*yaml"
  tags: [spec, implemented]
- label: The presets table in README lists exactly the pre-built configs shipped in the repo: example, tech-news, ml-ai, product-launches, release-tracker, academic-papers, video-podcast
  command: grep -q "config.example.yaml" README.md && grep -q "config.tech-news.yaml" README.md && grep -q "config.ml-ai.yaml" README.md && grep -q "config.academic-papers.yaml" README.md && grep -q "config.video-podcast.yaml" README.md
  tags: [spec, implemented]

# docker
- label: Docker image bundles all 7 config.*.yaml files under /app/presets/
  command: grep -q "COPY config.*.yaml /app/presets" Dockerfile
  tags: [spec, implemented]
- label: docker run image --preset tech-news works directly (no volume mount needed for preset)
  command: grep -q 'src/cli.ts' Dockerfile
  tags: [spec, implemented]
- docker run ghcr.io/av/pace:latest --preset tech-news starts the server using the tech-news preset config @spec @implemented
- docker run -v pace-data:/app/data ghcr.io/av/pace:latest starts successfully on first run and serves the dashboard (data dir is writable) @spec @implemented

